# XBD 4.1 Case Insensitive Comparisons
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# The rule is stated once for every utility and function that matches
# without regard to case. regcomp() with REG_ICASE and fnmatch() with
# FNM_CASEFOLD are tested in their own modules (XSH.regcomp.027,
# XSH.fnmatch.021 and .022); these assertions apply the same rule to
# utilities, in the POSIX locale.

assertion  XBD.4.1-case-insensitive-comparisons.001
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap04.html#4.1
anchor     utilities/grep.html#OPTIONS
class      required
says       grep -i, in the POSIX locale, selects a line when changing the
           case of any of its characters in a line that matches case-
           sensitively would still match: literal letters, bracket ranges,
           the lower and upper classes, back-references (both copies may
           differ in case) and, with -E, alternation and repetition; the
           same patterns without -i keep case significant.
test       t/001-grep-icase.sh

assertion  XBD.4.1-case-insensitive-comparisons.002
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap04.html#4.1
anchor     utilities/find.html#OPERANDS
class      required
says       find -iname matches a file name against a pattern with bracket
           ranges, classes and wildcards without regard to case (the
           counterpart rule), where -name with the same pattern does not.
test       t/002-find-iname.sh

assertion  XBD.4.1-case-insensitive-comparisons.003
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap04.html#4.1
anchor     utilities/sed.html#EXTENDED DESCRIPTION
class      required
says       The i flag of the sed s command matches literals, bracket
           ranges, classes and back-references without regard to case,
           in the POSIX locale; without the flag case matters.
test       t/003-sed-icase.sh
